@using QMgr.Web.Services

@*
    "Powered by SACC Software" — and THE ONE HOME for it.

    Six public pages rendered this line as their own literal <p class="powered-by">, and the
    signed-in shell rendered a different one ("© 2026 Q-Mgr Front-Office Platform"), and every
    outbound email a third. A tenant that pays to remove attribution and still meets our name on
    one of the three has not got what they bought, which is what happens the moment there are three
    copies of the decision. There is one now: this component, MainLayout's footer, and
    EmailTemplates all read FeatureCodes.RemoveAttribution and nothing else.

    It is NEVER the client's decision. The flag arrives already resolved — the entitlement, the
    tenant's own white-label switch and, on a public page, whether this host is actually a live
    tenant domain are all applied server-side — and it defaults to SHOWING the line, so a dropped
    request or an unreachable API leaves the attribution in place rather than quietly removing it.
*@
@if (!Hidden)
{
    <p class="powered-by">Powered by SACC Software</p>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// Which host this browser arrived on. Used on the PUBLIC pages, where nobody has signed in
    /// and the host is the only thing that identifies a tenant.
    /// </summary>
    [CascadingParameter] public TenantHostContext Host { get; set; } = TenantHostContext.Platform;

    /// <summary>
    /// Overrides the cascade, for the signed-in shell. There the source is the ORGANISATION's own
    /// entitlement rather than the host: a school that has paid for this should not also have to
    /// buy a domain to get it, and most of them sign in on the platform host.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool? Removed { get; set; }

    private bool Hidden => Removed ?? Host.AttributionRemoved;
}
